# Languages that need an extension

The editor ships no grammar for the languages on this card. The extension
knows each name, from the language list of GitHub, and names the grammar the
body needs. In a window with no other extension, only the line that opens the
fence is coloured. Install an extension for the language, and the body is
coloured too.

## By name

```kotlin
data class Language(val name: String, val year: Int) { val age get() = 2026 - year }
```

```zig
const Language = struct { name: []const u8, year: u16 };
```

```haskell
data Language = Language { name :: String, year :: Int } deriving Show
```

```toml
[language]
name = "HQL"
year = 2026
```

```graphql
query { languages(typing: STATIC) { name year } }
```

```elixir
defmodule Language do
  defstruct [:name, :year]
end
```

```ocaml
type language = { name : string; year : int }
```

```nix
{ pkgs ? import <nixpkgs> {} }: pkgs.mkShell { buildInputs = [ pkgs.cargo ]; }
```

```terraform
resource "null_resource" "language" { triggers = { year = "2026" } }
```

```protobuf
message Language { string name = 1; int32 year = 2; }
```

## By alias and by file extension

`kt` is a file extension only Kotlin uses, so it names Kotlin. `hs`, `ex` and
`tf` work the same way.

```kt#eval id=age
println(Language("Kotlin", 2011).age)
```

```hs#eval
main = print (2026 - 1990)
```

```ex#eval hidden
IO.puts(2026 - 2012)
```

```tf
variable "year" { default = 2026 }
```

## Diagrams

```mermaid
graph LR
  card --> cell --> output
```

```d2
card -> cell -> output
```

## No language at all

No language has this name. The extension still reads the line as a cell.

```nosuchlang#eval id=nothing
this body stays in one colour
```

```nosuchlang
and so does this one
```
